<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/13th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Saule</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1236">1236</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonian-crusade">Livonian Crusade</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Saule was fought on 22 September 1236 in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, near modern Šiauliai, when Samogitian and Semigallian forces destroyed an army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonian-brothers-of-the-sword">Livonian Brothers of the Sword</a> returning from a raid, killing the order's master <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volquin">Volkwin</a>. The broken order was merged into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> in 1237, becoming its Livonian branch.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-saule.jpg" alt="Battle of Saule" width="320" height="202" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rimantas Lazdynas · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2010_05_22_062Saul%C4%97sm%C5%AB%C5%A1is.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonian-brothers-of-the-sword">Livonian Brothers of the Sword</a> were established at Riga in 1202 to conquer and convert the pagan Baltic tribes, and were the first Catholic military order in the Baltic lands. By the 1230s, under Master Volkwin, the order had strained finances, falling numbers and a poor reputation.</p><p>On 19 February 1236 Gregory IX issued a bull declaring a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>. Volkwin was reluctant, since the order lacked the strength and knew little of the lands south of its territory, and he delayed for a whole summer. In the autumn a party of crusaders from Holstein arrived in Riga and demanded to be led into battle. Volkwin gathered a large force of troops from the Pskov Republic, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonians">Livonians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latgalians">Latgallians</a>, Curonians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonians">Estonians</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The crusaders marched south through Semigallian lands, which the order did not control, and raided Samogitian settlements, giving the locals only a few days to gather troops. On the way back north the knights met a determined group of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samogitians">Samogitians</a> at a river crossing. The Holsteiners, unwilling to risk their horses in the swampland, refused to fight on foot, and the army camped for the night.</p><p>On the day of Saint Maurice, 22 September 1236, the main pagan force, possibly led by Duke Vykintas, arrived. Lithuanian light cavalry threw javelins at short range, which told heavily against the unwieldy Livonian heavy cavalry, and the swamp favoured the lightly armed. The Livonian Rhymed Chronicle says the Master and his Brothers resisted until their horses were killed and then fought on foot. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volquin">Volkwin</a> and almost all the members of the order died. Those who fled towards Riga were allegedly killed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semigallians">Semigallians</a>, and only one crusader in ten reached the city.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Between 48 and 60 knights were killed. It was the earliest large-scale defeat suffered by the orders in the Baltic lands. The remnants of the Sword-Brothers accepted incorporation into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> in 1237. The battle inspired rebellions among the Curonians, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semigallians">Semigallians</a>, Selonians and Oeselians, tribes the order had conquered, and some thirty years of conquests on the left bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-dvina">Daugava</a> were reversed.</p><p>The exact site is unknown. It has traditionally been placed at Šiauliai or at Vecsaule near Bauska, and in 1965 Friedrich Benninghoven proposed the village of Jauniūnai, where the Lithuanian government sponsored a memorial in 2010. In 2000 the Lithuanian and Latvian parliaments declared 22 September Baltic Unity Day in commemoration.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Saule” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Pressure from the crusading orders encouraged unification: in 1236 a force of Samogitians and other Lithuanians annihilated the Livonian Brothers of the Sword at the <b>Battle of Saule</b>, the event from which the state is conventionally dated here.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1237 the Order also absorbed the remnants of the Livonian Brothers of the Sword, shattered by the Lithuanians at the <b>Battle of Saule</b>, and so gained a northern branch in Livonia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-the-teutonic-order">State of the Teutonic Order · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1236 a crusade against Lithuania ended in disaster at the <b>Battle of Saule</b>, where Samogitians and Semigallians killed Master Volquin and about fifty brothers.</blockquote><a href="/state/livonian-brothers-of-the-sword">Livonian Brothers of the Sword</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Livonian Brothers of the Sword were destroyed at <b>Saule</b> in 1236, the survivors were incorporated into the Teutonic Order in 1237, with Hermann Balk as first master in Livonia.</blockquote><a href="/state/livonian-order">Livonian Order</a></li><li><blockquote>Viesturs allied with the Germans of Riga against the Lithuanians early in the 13th century, then turned against them and joined the Lithuanian victory over the Livonian Brothers of the Sword at <b>Saule</b> in 1236.</blockquote><a href="/state/semigallians">Semigallians</a></li></ul></section>
